  "source_name": "https://www.whattoexpect.com/first-year/health-and-safety/newborn-screening-tests-and-procedures/",
  "source_type": "website",
  "structure": {
    "block_count": 68,
    "blocks": [
      {
        "block_id": "block_000000",
        "block_index": 0,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h1",
        "text": "Newborn Screenings: What Tests Will My Baby Get in the Hospital?",
        "text_sha256": "3898ca1228fb57e098588e4da2389e56a87a0e26943e477e1cf969b3c53acbea",
        "word_count": 11
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000001",
        "block_index": 1,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "newborn getting screened",
        "text_sha256": "7cd3efbdf1bf8bb5b4b4858314c38c5d8560f709965313294fe6b4831b1f48d3",
        "word_count": 3
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000002",
        "block_index": 2,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Believe it or not, your newborn will be tested for dozens of conditions in his first 48 hours of life.",
        "text_sha256": "f9391951372863e19a6708c89a6eb51f6aa5a214282ced0133d0aa6a0e65b9fe",
        "word_count": 20
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000003",
        "block_index": 3,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Though most babies are born healthy and stay that way, a very small percentage are apparently healthy at birth and then get sick because of a metabolic disorder — which can be potentially life-threatening if left undetected and untreated.",
        "text_sha256": "6cca70d8094608e15bffbb94ba43aba68597164bc5f382a1647e9fb863562de5",
        "word_count": 39
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000004",
        "block_index": 4,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Other babies are born with various rare but serious conditions, like a congenital heart condition. Luckily, inexpensive tests can catch all of these conditions early, and proper treatment can, in many cases, save lives.",
        "text_sha256": "2276651a17a82cadba873a2c4e1b9c1faa2ad30609d3cd494d4193a9f929a429",
        "word_count": 34
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000005",
        "block_index": 5,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "The following screening tests and procedures will make sure your little one gets the healthiest start to life.",
        "text_sha256": "2826fb2b82fff9893eddf9ad629800aa017e1dc7c271ccc470cad05b6bd4c246",
        "word_count": 18
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000006",
        "block_index": 6,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "How many tests and screenings will my newborn need in total?",
        "text_sha256": "7a30e7ab1856ff5cbe107df2ceda61c2052a76f429feb7e57656240cfc895d6b",
        "word_count": 11
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000007",
        "block_index": 7,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "The number of newborn screenings your baby will receive varies by state, but one blood draw that screens for at least 21 serious genetic, metabolic, hormonal and functional disorders is required by law. The U.S. Department of Health and Human Services (HHS) recommends screening for 35 core and 26 secondary conditions to check for a total of 61 different disorders.",
        "text_sha256": "b5c8cf5cf861f27e42a779ec480848fb250566e649369687c5b6ec0ba2428faa",
        "word_count": 60
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000008",
        "block_index": 8,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Your baby will also likely receive several other basic screenings designed to detect other issues, like congenital heart defects and impaired hearing.",
        "text_sha256": "ba5a6883e4fe880dd0f71d3d9db04bebabde354526fb0c2d1bf4574c0019650e",
        "word_count": 22
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000009",
        "block_index": 9,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "What kind of tests will my newborn baby get?",
        "text_sha256": "8ce9c2d2f4edaedf41de3abae2394e351decf9c2162c1cb21af9e917aea8f351",
        "word_count": 9
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000010",
        "block_index": 10,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "The newborn screenings your baby will most likely undergo include:",
        "text_sha256": "0394379e9f8f8d2daeb14562784ba24bfc4f1d9bba3365faf7e8af8886813831",
        "word_count": 10
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000011",
        "block_index": 11,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h3",
        "text": "A rating on the Apgar scale",
        "text_sha256": "d5fd52fb8ec6b7a1242daaa3fd9085ae67eef83d3a52dd265b5d63a351d4489e",
        "word_count": 6
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000012",
        "block_index": 12,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "This simple and essential assessment of baby’s condition is done between one and five minutes after birth.",
        "text_sha256": "a6460b454c6277b9dc3ac0d6abd2a3a6e924b93bc4e76984bf7ead691538e9fa",
        "word_count": 17
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000013",
        "block_index": 13,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h3",
        "text": "A congenital heart disease screening",
        "text_sha256": "0dee82f91d22b671b66e01ea4a7ca5ca7949f72169114a31c2b49969c65ba54f",
        "word_count": 5
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000014",
        "block_index": 14,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) also recommends, and some states require, screening tests soon after birth for congenital heart defects . This condition, which affects 1 in 100 babies, can lead to disability or death if not caught and treated in time.",
        "text_sha256": "b32ddd82265b5da6bd284e9070e7983580ae94519847d574c54666eaf4dfcbaa",
        "word_count": 45
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000015",
        "block_index": 15,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Happily, when a baby gets a diagnosis and treatment early, those risks are reduced significantly and, in most cases, completely.",
        "text_sha256": "4011a5ec0900cb535195fd6568a3f4114cffc5cd3c9e7f39c3cab90781f9d162",
        "word_count": 20
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000016",
        "block_index": 16,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h3",
        "text": "21 or more types of blood screens",
        "text_sha256": "8f4f492c1990d1ffbf70660042a4f560b22cb39524be2c40cd6b9e315dadbff6",
        "word_count": 7
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000017",
        "block_index": 17,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "A single blood test looks for at least 21 serious genetic, metabolic, hormonal and functional disorders, including PKU, hypothyroidism, congenital adrenal hyperplasia, biotinidase deficiency, maple syrup urine disease, galactosemia, homocystinuria, medium-chain acyl-CoA dehydrogenase deficiency and sickle cell anemia.",
        "text_sha256": "4c3f90a51457a8e66d347638f87a09bd53383866ebc013618f667affa4804b51",
        "word_count": 38
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000018",
        "block_index": 18,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Although most of these conditions are very rare, they can be life-threatening if they go undetected and untreated.",
        "text_sha256": "8ff4b4907a79910fb9bf3ff38d88215b37b1863ef823affdff2cb32d0b1a24b7",
        "word_count": 18
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000019",
        "block_index": 19,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h3",
        "text": "A hearing test",
        "text_sha256": "18c9f2ddcc46d4585937e297334bd53b43686f8e2f8ccc8ee18d4ac3f716a6f6",
        "word_count": 3
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000020",
        "block_index": 20,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "For 2 to 4 out of every 1,000 babies born each year in the U.S., the sense of hearing — so integral to the development of speech and language — is impaired.",
        "text_sha256": "f78b0d46e4eee8d44ef233dcd453fb51a74c54601cd6319c0285114cddec9d95",
        "word_count": 32
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000021",
        "block_index": 21,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Since a deficit can affect so many aspects of a child’s development, early diagnosis and treatment are key, which is why the American Academy of Pediatrics (AAP) endorses the test for all newborn babies.",
        "text_sha256": "64f7f6537b098cd11fd029067c088e3f806742c07b917b11012756925bf28d1e",
        "word_count": 34
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000022",
        "block_index": 22,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h3",
        "text": "A physical examination and preventive treatments",
        "text_sha256": "023f0f52c87a28a73f6647f3e5135f856abdbdec398d501e4dd53f207274991b",
        "word_count": 6
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000023",
        "block_index": 23,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "In addition to conducting the core newborn screening tests during your baby's hospital stay, your doctor, midwife or nurse will most likely also:",
        "text_sha256": "a1a80d38d30aa38de1dbdb0a5c4343bfe132fd71718b12e2043587de21b91919",
        "word_count": 23
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000024",
        "block_index": 24,
        "block_type": "li",
        "tag": "li",
        "text": "Weigh baby and measure his length and head circumference",
        "text_sha256": "991c650da28633e33c123640cc5f19b49d120046b73b8ecdb7328268e9fd6850",
        "word_count": 9
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000025",
        "block_index": 25,
        "block_type": "li",
        "tag": "li",
        "text": "Count fingers and toes and note if any body parts and features appear abnormal",
        "text_sha256": "51741d9e488b2ee35762e42395271ffe4be9bea4b7e85b49cdd64e6a408148ac",
        "word_count": 14
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000026",
        "block_index": 26,
        "block_type": "li",
        "tag": "li",
        "text": "Examine baby's internal organs, including kidneys, liver and spleen, externally by touch",
        "text_sha256": "4f1d81313498e5cd63587a9e3a009a5333513fe21a739d5a10e8e5c3882d8738",
        "word_count": 12
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000027",
        "block_index": 27,
        "block_type": "li",
        "tag": "li",
        "text": "Check your newborn's reflexes, hip rotation and umbilical stump",
        "text_sha256": "a9e886ff3f923d3580d7037eb01fa22794871b5b228ae855846d0deef7015f19",
        "word_count": 9
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000028",
        "block_index": 28,
        "block_type": "li",
        "tag": "li",
        "text": "Record your baby's first pees and poops to rule out elimination issues",
        "text_sha256": "9900c1972fbac51ea9b3f4cbe0f45ff23974abacb00ce23ad0d4206aacf849fc",
        "word_count": 12
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000029",
        "block_index": 29,
        "block_type": "li",
        "tag": "li",
        "text": "Administer antibiotic eye ointment to prevent infection, a vitamin K injection and the first dose of the hepatitis B vaccine sometime before hospital discharge",
        "text_sha256": "bdebdb3148152ee02bc487493990a349a14ee57b16296ff5df4b7b1aabc6d222",
        "word_count": 24
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000030",
        "block_index": 30,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "How is my baby tested?",
        "text_sha256": "0a838222c134c4e11416a4c5f3aaa34d3dc87170d7e6e3241f391fe87a3b474c",
        "word_count": 5
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000031",
        "block_index": 31,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Within the first 24 to 48 hours after birth, your baby will most likely undergo the following procedures:",
        "text_sha256": "0f5626e4bd09278392cbc6f144ccc27f0f1bfc1d47a71090e73ce910c7f430aa",
        "word_count": 18
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000032",
        "block_index": 32,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h3",
        "text": "Blood test",
        "text_sha256": "7d189334dfbae8f774917f8aa8f582d3fcbfe131694d788cef0b3cc2d1855257",
        "word_count": 2
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000033",
        "block_index": 33,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "To test for more than 21 possible metabolic, genetic, hormonal and functional disorders, a few drops of blood are routinely taken using a single heel stick test usually 24 to 48 hours after birth.",
        "text_sha256": "fef20879bf05687eee37b86aa8818a78169700549a2ea08a137ef07bb0ab8b49",
        "word_count": 34
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000034",
        "block_index": 34,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h3",
        "text": "Congenital heart disease screening",
        "text_sha256": "8ee731d7fd9f6a58d4b5edc3cd76f917262c9cb4b1c734223cceb238dc2bd9c6",
        "word_count": 4
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000035",
        "block_index": 35,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "This screening, using pulse oximetry, is simple and painless. A sensor is placed on your baby’s skin to measure your little one’s pulse and the amount of oxygen in his blood.",
        "text_sha256": "134baef085db6ef4ab8088221b850e060cc5cab86657da1643592ab448d5f40e",
        "word_count": 31
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000036",
        "block_index": 36,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "If the results of the screening seem questionable, the doctor will request further testing (like an echocardiogram or an ultrasound of the heart) to determine if anything is wrong.",
        "text_sha256": "bafef72565b9e71931c242946ce87ca6f0c54d739fe0cb4668de03811e2cdbc6",
        "word_count": 29
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000037",
        "block_index": 37,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h3",
        "text": "Newborn hearing screening",
        "text_sha256": "2acd51e2352f2aa3927b2c51e7fa301aa78494fa1c514d720ff6cd82ab3befce",
        "word_count": 3
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000038",
        "block_index": 38,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "One test, called otoacoustic emissions (OAE), measures response to sound by using a small probe inserted in baby’s ear canal.",
        "text_sha256": "4db0b0503c1b5edbd9e2fe7753eaaa273380eb53706ae0e24cbe0bc5f5c8a549",
        "word_count": 20
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000039",
        "block_index": 39,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "In babies with normal hearing, a microphone inside the probe records faint noises coming from the baby’s ear in response to a noise produced by the probe. The test can be done while baby is sleeping, is completed within a few minutes and causes no pain or discomfort.",
        "text_sha256": "e19fcc3e40289ee96e00c7fba3981e1a3cf5cefc64086a38cae7fb5b17d42e66",
        "word_count": 48
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000040",
        "block_index": 40,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "A second screening method, called auditory brainstem response (ABR), uses electrodes placed on baby’s scalp to detect activity in the brain stem’s auditory region in response to \"clicks\" sounded in the newborn's ear.",
        "text_sha256": "ed116396dd8a4c96cc1b7718a047068a2e3eb857661b35df67011d263646b62f",
        "word_count": 33
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000041",
        "block_index": 41,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "ABR screening requires the infant to be awake and in a quiet state, but it’s also quick and painless. If your baby doesn’t pass the initial screening, the test will be repeated to avoid false-positive results.",
        "text_sha256": "6eb0a323fd21b2324d2fccc1ed098ed6ef6e70fcd85fbc93a266d161c25380c9",
        "word_count": 36
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000042",
        "block_index": 42,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "What is the Apgar test?",
        "text_sha256": "34e4e57a5dff194d9dd4af6f844caf363dc08e5e6de874f8d6a2280ff9207290",
        "word_count": 5
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000043",
        "block_index": 43,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "The Apgar test is the first screening most newborns are given; the scores — rated on a scale of 0 to 10, and taken one minute and again five minutes after birth — reflect baby’s general condition.",
        "text_sha256": "15b3d02bc5be426f84280a0b2f84a6dec19c7d50e0349964d84dfac465d53664",
        "word_count": 37
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000044",
        "block_index": 44,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "They’re based on observations made in five assessment categories: appearance, pulse, reflexes, muscle tone and breathing.",
        "text_sha256": "9c90f42de5a48c8dc36eb34c95ce6c20c6b0df6716af10c02e02a74d9a593db2",
        "word_count": 16
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000045",
        "block_index": 45,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "What is the vitamin K shot?",
        "text_sha256": "09e6410907492e649e6eb692f96761d6b06a19520067bb20b5cda8cd5624e8b9",
        "word_count": 6
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000046",
        "block_index": 46,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Since most babies are born with low levels of vitamin K , they'll get a vitamin K shot within six hours of birth to enhance their blood-clotting ability and prevent a potentially dangerous condition from cropping up.",
        "text_sha256": "1f4bbe4f6df9a75e291f0ce7294cd13f395a00a980790add703001715e1d7664",
        "word_count": 37
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000047",
        "block_index": 47,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "Will my baby require every test? Can I opt out of newborn screening tests?",
        "text_sha256": "061f0a623ed16e90450ac517e9658052738a81b6533d7235ff13398c37a58015",
        "word_count": 14
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000048",
        "block_index": 48,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "All 50 states and the District of Columbia require a newborn blood screening for at least 21 disorders, with many states screening newborns for the 61 disorders suggested by the HHS and sometimes a few additional ones.",
        "text_sha256": "a597833469f5ab02cd6f8c16bd15914726d455f6e415ee43e25cb77bf8e8ea10",
        "word_count": 37
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000049",
        "block_index": 49,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Check with your practitioner or your local board of health to find out what tests are done in your state, or look them up online at Baby's First Test (a newborn screening clearinghouse funded by the Maternal and Child Health Bureau, a branch of the Health Resources & Services Administration).",
        "text_sha256": "8f275abdf89b7d559daa5b58353af8a86dbc91db0cf72ad702d067c5ab38d41e",
        "word_count": 50
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000050",
        "block_index": 50,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "If you’re concerned, discuss your fears at a prenatal visit — don’t wait until delivery day to speak up. You do have the right to opt out of any test or procedure, but you will need to sign paperwork documenting your decision.",
        "text_sha256": "76a221063fecc43b635a4420872ed3d351aa6631faf9a2fca559f25ab9d9e241",
        "word_count": 42
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000051",
        "block_index": 51,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "In some cases, the hospital might inform the state’s department of child services that you’ve decided to forgo the recommended screening.",
        "text_sha256": "f0555151475d49ce1f24eb104bfee060c0ff81f61a08cca6fd8ec50bbcc8983a",
        "word_count": 21
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000052",
        "block_index": 52,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Keep in mind, although most of these conditions are very rare, they can be life-threatening if they go undetected and untreated. Testing is inexpensive, and in the very unlikely event that your baby tests positive for any condition, the pediatrician can verify the results and begin treatment immediately, making a huge difference in the prognosis.",
        "text_sha256": "a053b773fe996165dfb4db91faaafdd3957f9a50fcb2aa470fe00a93452b1a19",
        "word_count": 55
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000053",
        "block_index": 53,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "It's in your child's best interest to get all the screenings your pediatrician and hospital recommend.",
        "text_sha256": "edaef4f5368d9fd88dfd92a1fe672b7a0d0ba169084e26fc902f0bc00926be00",
        "word_count": 16
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000054",
        "block_index": 54,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "Will my baby need more tests than the required screenings?",
        "text_sha256": "349c92d3220d8f1bb15fe4f0af9949b488d514b3f6b6ba661dd903d794a85554",
        "word_count": 10
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000055",
        "block_index": 55,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Certain states require additional \"secondary\" newborn screenings in their heel-stick tests, sometimes because of an at-risk population in the area. To check which conditions must be tested for in your state, visit Baby's First Test .",
        "text_sha256": "6f2992d302cace6f5309353a1ba0507758c3b68ce93075a250333fc54028ea56",
        "word_count": 36
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000056",
        "block_index": 56,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "Will my insurance cover all the newborn screening tests?",
        "text_sha256": "3453d74946f9652c266891c7cab5090a526b9d09a37172b611223b31725aef86",
        "word_count": 9
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000057",
        "block_index": 57,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Under the Affordable Care Act, your insurance is required to cover the following newborn screenings:",
        "text_sha256": "0a8d3d8f3ee8cbb6a827166a64ae3cd7f4e4a75288c4dbc3338e269695c75a13",
        "word_count": 15
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000058",
        "block_index": 58,
        "block_type": "li",
        "tag": "li",
        "text": "Congenital hypothyroidism",
        "text_sha256": "a7ddb03adb7169b64f3fcf0ee59729245e9b925d08fcd117138bad7c165a674e",
        "word_count": 2
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000059",
        "block_index": 59,
        "block_type": "li",
        "tag": "li",
        "text": "Sickle cell anemia",
        "text_sha256": "47d619162670ad6bc38e88cdf521d734e9d0f6711a2bada71296991ab88d04cd",
        "word_count": 3
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000060",
        "block_index": 60,
        "block_type": "li",
        "tag": "li",
        "text": "PKU",
        "text_sha256": "3c575aaf83860feddd08a29492f1daaecfbb80244decfe8ae3cc8cd42f523522",
        "word_count": 1
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000061",
        "block_index": 61,
        "block_type": "li",
        "tag": "li",
        "text": "Hearing screening",
        "text_sha256": "790e4b5ac8f47b1d85311aac24a2bf5acc9a8ab49bd87129caa0304527778630",
        "word_count": 2
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000062",
        "block_index": 62,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "There’s a good chance your insurance will cover all the other blood tests as well as the one for congenital heart defects, so be sure to check.",
        "text_sha256": "a7143b71735c5276033b985dc4cd36e341b0437e1362430347f02fa10a0e7a97",
        "word_count": 27
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000063",
        "block_index": 63,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "Can I ask for more newborn screening tests?",
        "text_sha256": "56d26e8e9dfb504f57eda25960c0022f8eeeea2078e6fb46a3caaff02947d9aa",
        "word_count": 8
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000064",
        "block_index": 64,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "If your hospital doesn’t automatically provide all 35 core newborn blood tests recommended by the HHS, you can arrange to have them done. Talk to your doctor, then check with your insurance to calculate your out-of-pocket costs, if any.",
        "text_sha256": "58e8f78383611e2962fd299efd21c76a409e3d57bc24968100d45b9d907810a5",
        "word_count": 39
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000065",
        "block_index": 65,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "If your state doesn’t require screening for congenital heart disease, ask your baby’s pediatrician if your little one can get tested.",
        "text_sha256": "52cb10196a9b0e0de0b9fded25892d086ff3b4ac29d85b0e99cbe763f9a28902",
        "word_count": 21
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000066",
        "block_index": 66,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "Are all newborn tests safe?",
        "text_sha256": "2b74cd3b3a20b4f796ae917c2be0170f2d20dbf40c4786417078bd5476dcf8c0",
        "word_count": 5
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000067",
        "block_index": 67,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Yes. All newborn tests have a proven track record of protecting babies and were recommended only after extensive research. They’re not only perfectly safe for your little one, they’re highly recommended.",
        "text_sha256": "1b379674ef4155d45f6e44aaa212db89117adf1200dd8266bb885360c0022585",
        "word_count": 31
      }
    ],
    "content_word_count": 1379,
    "heading_count": 19
  },
  "title": "Newborn Screenings: What Tests Will My Baby Get in the Hospital?",
